Comprehensive issues list (grouped, with file references & fixes)

A. Project structure and packaging (critical → will block imports on Linux)

Missing package init files

Problem: BackEnd directory (GrabNGo/BackEnd) is treated like a module but may be missing init.py.
Files: GrabNGo/BackEnd/*
Why: Without init.py, relative imports (from .Buyer_Login import ...) may fail, or import paths will be ambiguous.
Fix: Add empty init.py to GrabNGo and GrabNGo/BackEnd (even if empty).
Inconsistent folder / file naming across project

Problem: Frontend folder is GrapNGo_Frontend but other names use GrabNGo. Duplicate entrypoints: root GrabNGo.py and GrapNGo_Frontend/GrapNGo.py.
Why: Confusing, risk of running wrong entrypoint or wrong imports; also possible import failures.
Fix: Standardize naming. Either rename folder to GrabNGo_Frontend or edit imports and entrypoints to refer consistently to the existing folder name.
B. Import errors & case sensitivity (critical)

Case mismatches and bare module imports

Problem: Many backend imports use lower-case module names or non-relative imports, e.g. in GrabNGo/BackEnd/api.py:
from buyer_login import Buyer_Login
from seller_transactions import (Seller_View, ...) But files are named Buyer_Login.py, Seller_Transaction.py, etc.
Why: On Linux (case-sensitive FS) "buyer_login" != "Buyer_Login" → ImportError.
Fix: Use package-relative imports and correct module names. Example at top of api.py:
from .Buyer_Login import Buyer_Login
from .Seller_Transaction import Seller_View, Seller_Store_Manager, Seller_Account_Manager
Also ensure init.py exists.
Duplicate imports and inconsistent names

Problem: api.py imports FastAPI multiple times; imports of delivery agent classes differ across files (Delivery_Manager vs Delivery_agent manager).
Why: Ugly but not fatal; might reflect inconsistent class names across files.
Fix: Consolidate imports, use consistent names across modules.
C. Import-time side effects (critical → will run when module imported)

Database creation/population at import

Files:
GrabNGo/BackEnd/Database_Manipulation.py — creates con = Get_Database_connection() at module scope and executes Create_Database(con) and commit at import time (module end).
GrabNGo/BackEnd/main.py — executes populate_sample_data() unconditionally at import time.
Why: When you import any of these modules from api.py (or any other code) the DB will be created/populated and connections closed. This will cause data manipulations and may lock DBs on cloud hosts and cause unexpected side-effects.
Fix:
Move DB creation/population code into if name == "main": block or separate CLI function.
Remove or guard module-level con creation; expose a Get_Database_connection() function that callers use to create per-request connections.
Example: if name == "main": con = Get_Database_connection() ...create/populate... con.close()
uvicorn.run executed on import (possible in api.py)

Problem: If uvicorn.run is at module top-level, importing api will try to run server.
Fix: Ensure uvicorn.run or run block is under if name == "main".
D. Database schema & SQL issues (high)

Duplicate/incorrect column definitions in CREATE TABLE

File: GrabNGo/BackEnd/Database_Manipulation.py
Problem: In BUYER_ACCOUNTS CREATE TABLE: Password TEXT NOT NULL NOT NULL — repeated NOT NULL.
Why: Malformed SQL (double NOT NULL) might be rejected by sqlite or cause warnings.
Fix: Remove duplicate NOT NULL: Password TEXT NOT NULL,
Column name inconsistencies (case / underscore vs camel)

Problem: SELLER_PRODUCT uses column Product_Image_path in CREATE TABLE: Product_Image_path TEXT NOT NULL
But elsewhere update queries use Product_Image_Path (capital P and capital P) or Product_Image_Path spelled inconsistently.
Example update in Update_Table.update_entire_product uses Product_Image_Path (capital P).
Why: SQLite column names are case-insensitive but your code uses different names in code generation strings; more importantly some SQL strings reference Product_Image_Path with different capitalization and some Python code expects the field index in tuples. Inconsistent names make it hard to reason about and may cause missing data if code writes/reads different keys.
Fix: Standardize column name to Product_Image_path everywhere (or Product_Image_path consistently). Update all SQL statements to match.
Wrong SQL parameter placeholder / syntax

Files & lines:
Display_From_Table.display_delivery_agent_history: self.cus.execute("""SELECT * FROM HISTORY WHERE Delivery_Agent_Id=ID? """, (ID,)) This is incorrect — 'ID?' is not a placeholder.
Many queries have odd spacing or missing parentheses for OR precedence in get_unit_time: SELECT Avg_Time_Minutes FROM NEIGHBORHOOD_DELIVERY WHERE Neighborhood_A =? AND Neighborhood_B=? OR Neighborhood_A=? AND Neighborhood_B=? This needs parentheses to ensure correct logical grouping.
Why:
"ID?" breaks SQL and will raise sqlite3.OperationalError: near "ID": syntax error.
OR precedence without parentheses gives unintended results; you want (A AND B) OR (B AND A).
Fix:
Change to: SELECT * FROM HISTORY WHERE Delivery_Agent_Id = ?
For unit_time: wrap groups: WHERE (Neighborhood_A = ? AND Neighborhood_B = ?) OR (Neighborhood_A = ? AND Neighborhood_B = ?)
Logic bug in get_free_agent — fetchone in wrong place

File: Database_Manipulation.py -> get_free_agent
Problem: Code executes SELECT Agent_Name inside a for loop for each seller_id, but fetchone() is called only once AFTER the loop (name=self.cus.fetchone()). As written, only the last executed SELECT's fetchone will be retrieved; the code actually iterates but never checks each fetchone properly.
Why: It yields unpredictable results and likely returns None or wrong agent.
Fix: Move fetchone() inside loop or better: query Delivery_Agent table in a single query JOIN to find first matching active agent.
Example fix: for seller_id in data: self.cus.execute("SELECT Agent_Name FROM DELIVERY_AGENT WHERE Delivery_Agent_Id = ? AND Status = 'Active'", (seller_id,)) name = self.cus.fetchone() if name: return name[0]
Some SELECTs use fetchall() then treat results as single rows or assume specific indices

Problem: Several methods return lists or single values inconsistently (e.g., get_buyer_info uses fetchall and returns data (list of rows) while many consumers expect a single row).
Why: Caller functions may assume a single tuple and use row[0] etc., causing IndexError if returned data shape unexpected.
Fix: Standardize getters: use fetchone() when expecting a single row and return None or dict/tuple consistently. Add docstrings.
Wrong attribute/column mapping in display_product_details_by_product_id_and_seller_id

File: Database_Manipulation.py
Problem: product = [row[2], row[3], row[4], row[5], row[6]] but SELECT * returns a particular order; if column order changes these indices are fragile. Also the table SELLER_PRODUCT's column order per CREATE: Seller_ID(0), Product_ID(1), Description(2), Category(3), Price(4), Quantity(5), Product_Image_path(6)
Why: The code later uses indices elsewhere; fragile mapping may cause wrong values to be displayed.
Fix: Use named columns (sqlite3.Row row_factory) or destructure by positions with comments and consistent mapping.
Mistaken update SQL column name in Update_Table.update_entire_product

File: Database_Manipulation.py
Problem: SQL uses Product_Image_Path (capital P) whereas CREATE used Product_Image_path.
Fix: make consistent.
Wrong parameter order in update_seller_product_price

File: Database_Manipulation.py -> update_seller_product_price(self, id1, id2, price): self.cus.execute("""UPDATE SELLER_PRODUCT SET Price=? WHERE Product_ID=? AND Seller_ID=?""", (price, id2, id1))
Problem: The SQL uses (price, id2, id1) but parameters named id1 (maybe seller_id) id2 (maybe product_id) — ambiguous naming. This is probably correct in intent, but function parameter names are unclear and callers may pass swapped values.
Fix: Rename to update_seller_product_price(self, seller_id, product_id, price) and pass (price, product_id, seller_id).
